## Part 2: Political Decay & Constitutional Collapse (1951-1958)

### A. Institutional Breakdown

| Period | Event | Significance |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 1951-58 | **7 Prime Ministers** | Power shifted from politicians to civil-military bureaucracy |
| 1953 | **Nazimuddin dismissed** | Governor-General Ghulam Mohammad ousted PM with majority — extra-constitutional precedent |
| 1954 | **Constituent Assembly dissolved** | Constitutional coup validated by courts under "doctrine of necessity" — recurring legal theme |
| 1953 | **Anti-Ahmadiyya Riots** | Punjab violence; first martial law (Lahore); **Munir Report** critiqued religion-state mixing |
| Early 1950s | **Food shortages** | Partition disrupted irrigation; refugee influx strained resources |
| 1955 | **One Unit Scheme** | Merged West Pakistan provinces for parity with East Pakistan; centralized power, fueled regional resentment (Sindh, Balochistan) |

### B. Constitutional Farce & Military Takeover

**1956 Constitution** — finally passed after 9 years:
- Declared **Islamic Republic**
- Established parliamentary system
- **No general election ever held** under it

**General Iskander Mirza** (first President):
- Schemed rival factions
- **October 7, 1958** — abrogated constitution, imposed Martial Law, appointed **Ayub Khan** as CMLA
- **October 27, 1958** — Ayub exiled Mirza; military rule consolidated

