## Part 4: Other Critical Developments

### A. Bengali Language Movement (1948-1952)

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Issue** | Urdu declared sole national language — alienated Bengali-majority East Pakistan |
| **Culmination** | **Feb 21, 1952** — police fired on Dhaka University protesters |
| **Legacy** | Now **UNESCO International Mother Language Day**; first crack in East-West unity |

### B. Afghanistan Relations
- Only UN member to vote against Pakistan's admission (1947)
- Disputed **Durand Line**; supported **Pakhtunistan** movement
- Persistent western border tension

### C. Civil Service Dominance
- **CSP** (Civil Service of Pakistan) inherited British ICS tradition
- Bureaucrats (Ghulam Mohammad, Chaudhry Mohammad Ali) wielded extraordinary power
- Established civil-military dominance over elected politicians — enduring pattern

---

## Central Theme: Failure of Democratic Consolidation

| Factor | Consequence |
|--------|-------------|
| Jinnah's death (1948) | Removed unifying national figure |
| Liaquat's assassination (1951) | Eliminated capable successor |
| Triangular power struggle | Weak politicians vs. powerful bureaucrats vs. resourced military |
| Military victory (1958) | Complete collapse of parliamentary experiment |

**Every major event** — constitutional delays, One Unit, assembly dismissals — connects to institutional fragility.

